===== Backup =====

Please backup your original firmware!
\\
\\
=== Tools ===
   * [[https://github.com/rvalles/brntool]]
   * [[http://code.google.com/p/brndumper/]]
\\
I will use brntool to explain the backup here.
As an alternative you could use brndumper to save the original firmware (If you prefer a graphical interface, brndumper is probably better for you).
\\
\\
=== brntool ===

  * According to the readme of brntool
    * A successful flash block read will output the address and size of the block while a botched one (a byte or more gets lost in the serial port) will output '!' and retry. Even so, unless in a hurry, I'd recommend to at least dump twice and compare the dumps, just to be on the safe side.
  * A memory dump of the original firmware is done in about one hour
    * **You should dump the original firmware at least twice (as recommended by the author of brntool), so the whole backup process takes about two hours.**
\\
  - Download the small python script [[https://raw.githubusercontent.com/rvalles/brntool/master/brntool.py]]
  - Install the python package pyserial [[https://pyserial.readthedocs.io/en/latest/pyserial.html#installation]]
  - Enter the "Administrator Mode" of the DANUBE bootloader (OEM bootloader) of your device
    - Connect to the serial header ([[arv752dpw#Open up and connect cable]])
    - Open a terminal and type <code>screen /dev/ttyUSB0 115200</code>
    - Power on the device and quickly press the space bar 3 times
    - Enter the "Administration Mode" in the DANUBE bootloader by typing an exclamation mark <code>!</code>
    - You should see something like this <code>
Press Space Bar 3 times to enter command mode ...123
Yes, Enter command mode ...


[DANUBE Boot]:!

Enter Administrator Mode !

======================
 [#] Set Serial Number
 [2] Use Normal Firmware
 [3] Use ART-Testing Firmware
 [9] Taggle ART Firmware Enable/Disable
 [A] Set MAC Address
 [E] Erase Flash
 [G] Run Runtime Code
 [H] Set Options
 [M] Upload to Memory
 [P] Print Boot Params
 [R] Read from Memory
 [T] Memory Test
 [U] Upload to Flash
 [V] Set Board Version
 [W] Write to Memory
 [Y] Go to Memory
 [Z] Dump DDR Ram Register
 [0] Primary = Image 0
 [1] Primary = Image 1
======================

[DANUBE Boot]:
</code>
    - Close your serial connection by pressing the key combination in the terminal <code>[CTRL] + [A] then [K] then [Y] to confirm exit</code>
  - With the serial connection closed, you can now dump the original firmware
    - Dump the orginal firmware by typing <code>python3 brntool.py --read=ARV752DPW_1.dump --addr=0xB0000000 --verbose --size=0x800000</code> **0xB0000000** is the starting address of flash memory
    - Now you should have a file called "ARV752DPW_1.dump"
    - Restart the device and enter the "Administrator Mode" again, so you can dump the original firmware the second time
    - Dump the orginal firmware a second time by typing <code>python3 brntool.py --read=ARV752DPW_2.dump --addr=0xB0000000 --verbose --size=0x800000</code>
    - Now you should have a file called "ARV752DPW_2.dump"
    - List both files by typing <code>ls -l ARV752DPW_*.dump</code> **You should see two files listed, if this is not the case please rerun the backup process** <code>
**Example output:**
-rw-r--r--. 1 root root 8388608 12. Feb 23:32 ARV752DPW_1.dump
-rw-r--r--. 1 root root 8388608 13. Feb 00:58 ARV752DPW_2.dump
</code>
    - Check if the files have any difference by typing <code>diff ARV752DPW_1.dump ARV752DPW_2.dump</code> **You should see no output, if this is not the case please rerun the backup process**
    - Compare both files by typing <code>cmp ARV752DPW_1.dump ARV752DPW_2.dump</code> **You should see no output, if this is not the case please rerun the backup process**
    - Finally we generate the md5 hash of both files. Type:<code>md5sum ARV752DPW_*</code> **You should see the hashes of both files, please compare them. If they are not the same, please perform the backup process again**<code>
**Example output:**
b245fc54da24db7a81bb915e968453f8  ARV752DPW_1.dump
b245fc54da24db7a81bb915e968453f8  ARV752DPW_2.dump
</code>
    - If everything is ok you will end with two identical files
  - You now have a full backup of the original firmware



==== Restore backup ====

You need a working U-Boot bootloader running in RAM

  - Make sure your U-Boot bootloader is working
  - Get the size of the baackup by typing in your linux terminal <code>printf "%x\n" `stat -c "%s" "<backup file>"`</code>
  - Uploading backup to RAM
    - Quick way: xmodem
      - Calculate crc32 checksum of the backup by typing <code>crc32 <backup file></code> Maybe requires additional package e.g. for Ubuntu install package ''libarchive-zip-perl''
      - Open a serial console by typing <code>screen /dev/ttyUSB0 115200</code>
      - Start the xmodem upload mode of your device by typing <code>loadx</code>
      - Close the serial connection by pressing the key combination in the terminal <code>[CTRL] + [A] then [K] then [Y] to confirm exit</code>
      - Upload the backup from your local computer to the RAM of the device by typing <code>sx <backup file> </dev/ttyUSB0 >/dev/ttyUSB0</code> Maybe requires additional package e.g. for Ubuntu install package ''lrzsz''
      - After the transfer is complete, open a serial console again by typing <code>screen /dev/ttyUSB0 115200</code>
      - Open the menu again by hitting <code>[ENTER]</code>
    - Slow way: write-to-uboot.py
      - Open a serial console by typing <code>screen /dev/ttyUSB0 115200</code>
        - You should see the U-Boot command line <code>ARV752DPW #</code>
        - If not, hit <code>[ENTER]</code> If nothing happens and the serial console is not responding, reboot your device and load the temporary U-Boot again.
      - Close your serial connection by pressing the key combination in the terminal <code>[CTRL] + [A] then [K] then [Y] to confirm exit</code>
      - Download the Python script https://raw.githubusercontent.com/Yemtex/OpenWrt_Arcadyan-ARV752DPW/main/write-to-uboot.py
        - Old version, does not work with python3 [[http://blog.galax.is/files/802/write-to-uboot.py]]
      - Transfer your image to your device via serial connection by using the downloaded Python script. Type <code>python3 write-to-uboot.py --serial=/dev/ttyUSB0 --write=<backup file> --addr=0x81000000 --verbose</code>
      - The output of the executed script shows what to do next <code>**Example output**
File successfully written. You should run command 'crc32 81000000 2e974' on the modem and the result must be f6901891.
</code>
  - Open a serial console if not already open by typing <code>screen /dev/ttyUSB0 115200</code>
  - Calculate the crc32 checksum of the uploaded image by typing <code>crc32 81000000 <size of image></code> Replace ''<size of image>'' with your calculated size
  - The result should be exactly the same as your previously calculated checksum \\ **If the checksum is not the same, the uploaded image is corrupted. Please try to upload it again.**
  - Unprotect the sectors by typing <code>protect off all</code>
  - Erase the flash memory by typing <code>erase all</code>
  - Copy the uploaded backup from RAM to flash memory by typing <code>cp.b 0x81000000 0xb0000000 <size of image></code> Replace ''<size of image>'' with your calculated size
  - Calculate the crc32 checksum of the copied image by typing <code>crc32 b0000000 <size of image></code> Replace ''<size of image>'' with your calculated size
  - Protect all sectors by typing <code>protect on all</code>
  - Reboot device by typing <code>reset</code>



===== U-Boot Bootloader =====

General explanation:
  * [[docs:techref:bootloader]]
  * [[docs:techref:bootloader:uboot]]
  * [[docs:techref:bootloader:uboot.config]]
\\
^ U-Boot version ^ Purpose ^
| u-boot-..._brn    | Meant to be loaded from brnboot (stock bootloader) as a 2nd stage bootloader into RAM |
| u-boot-..._nor    | Meant to be flashed onto flash memory as main bootloader (at 0xB0000000), overwriting orignal bootloader |
| u-boot-..._ram    | Meant to be uploaded via UART by serial if nor_bootloader got bricked, for rescue purposes |
\\
The installation of the U-Boot bootloader is recommended and requires two steps. This will overwrite the original "Danube" bootloader.

  - Booting U-Boot from RAM as a second stage bootloader
    - [[arv752dpw#Temporary U-Boot]]
  - Flashing U-Boot permanently as the main bootloader
    - [[arv752dpw#Permanent U-Boot]]
