Environment size: 906/8188 bytes
ARV752DPW22 #</code>
        - ipaddr is the IP of the device (serving as the client, who is pulling the image from the server)
        - serverip is the IP of the NIC of your computer
      - Set the IP of your computer's NIC to the server IP e.g. ''192.168.1.2''
      - Start your tftp server on your computer
      - Calculate crc32 checksum of the OpenWrt initramfs-kernel image in a seperate Linux terminal by typing <code>crc32 <initramfs-kernel bin file></code> **Replace <initramfs-kernel bin file>**\\ Maybe requires additional package e.g. for Ubuntu install package ''libarchive-zip-perl''
      - Write the OpenWrt initramfs-kernel image to RAM by typing <code>tftpboot 0x81000000 <initramfs-kernel bin file></code> **Replace <initramfs-kernel bin file>**\\
    - Slow way: write-to-uboot.py
      - Open a serial console by typing <code>screen /dev/ttyUSB0 115200</code>
        - You should see the U-Boot command line <code>ARV752DPW #</code>
        - If not, hit <code>[ENTER]</code> If nothing happens and the serial console is not responding, reboot your device.
      - Close your serial connection by pressing the key combination in the terminal <code>[CTRL] + [A] then [K] then [Y] to confirm exit</code>
      - Download the Python script https://raw.githubusercontent.com/Yemtex/OpenWrt_Arcadyan-ARV752DPW/main/write-to-uboot.py
        - Old version, does not work with python3 [[http://blog.galax.is/files/802/write-to-uboot.py]]
      - Transfer your image to your device via serial connection by using the downloaded Python script. Type for example <code>python3 write-to-uboot.py --serial=/dev/ttyUSB0 --write=<initramfs-kernel bin file> --addr=0x81000000 --verbose</code> **Replace <initramfs-kernel bin file>**\\
      - The output of the executed script shows what to do next <code>**Example output**
File successfully written. You should run command 'crc32 81000000 4f3fe7' on the modem and the result must be a0a76a18.
</code>
  - Open a serial console if not already open by typing <code>screen /dev/ttyUSB0 115200</code>      
  - Calculate the crc32 checksum of the uploaded image by typing <code>crc32 81000000 <size of image></code> Replace ''<size of image>'' with your calculated size
  - The result should be exactly the same as your previously calculated checksum \\ **If the checksum is not the same, the uploaded image is corrupted. Please try to upload it again.**
  - Check if uploaded image is recognized as OpenWrt by typing <code>iminfo 0x81000000</code> **If the image has no header, there is no output** \\ **If the image is not recognized, the uploaded image is corrupted. Upload it again.** 
  - Boot uploaded image by typing <code>bootm</code>
  - Check if OpenWrt works as expected e.g. web interface LuCi

